When multiplying two matrices in general, we apply the same approach as usual:

- First, we verify that the two matrices are conformable for multiplication.
- Then, we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second.

For example, let's find $AB$, where

<div class="matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked">
<div>$A= \begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 & 3\end{bmatrix}$</div>
<div>$B= \begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\ 2 & -2 \\ -4 & 1\end{bmatrix}.$</div>
</div>

First, we verify the conformability for multiplication:

$$
\underset{\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}2}} \times \color{#2E8B57}{\fbox{3}}}{A} \underset{\color{#2E8B57}\checkmark}{\cdot} \underset{{\color{#2E8B57}\fbox{3}} \times \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}2}}}{B} = 
	\underset{\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}2}} \times \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}2}}}{C}
$$

So the matrices are conformable for multiplication, and the product is a $2 \times 2$ matrix. Let's set up the multiplication:

$$
\begin{aligned}&\underbrace{
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -2 & 2 & 1 \\
	1 & -1 & 3 
    \end{bmatrix}}_{A}

	\underbrace{
    \begin{bmatrix}
        -5 & 2  \\
	2 & -2  \\
	-4 & 1 
    \end{bmatrix}}_{B} \\ &=\underbrace{
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \ast & \ast  \\
        \ast & \ast 
    \end{bmatrix}
	}_{C}\end{aligned}
$$

Now, we calculate the entries of the product matrix (shown as $\ast$'s in the equation above).

To find $c_{11}$, the entry at the intersection of the first row and first column, we calculate the dot product of the first row of $A$ and the first column of $B$:

$$
\begin{aligned}&\begin{bmatrix}
        \color{#4169E1}-2 & \color{#4169E1}2 & \color{#4169E1}1 \\
	\color{gray}1 & \color{gray}-1 & \color{gray}3 
    \end{bmatrix}

    \begin{bmatrix}
         \color{red}-5 & \color{gray}2  \\
	\color{red}2 & \color{gray}-2  \\
	\color{red}-4 & \color{gray}1
    \end{bmatrix} \\ &=\begin{bmatrix}
        \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}10}} & \ast  \\
        \ast & \ast
    \end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}10}} = ({\color{#4169E1}{-2}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + {\color{#4169E1}{2}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{#4169E1}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}})
$$

To find $c_{12}$, we calculate the dot product of the first row of $A$ and the second column of $B$:

$$
\begin{aligned}&\begin{bmatrix}
        \color{#4169E1}-2 & \color{#4169E1}2 & \color{#4169E1}1 \\
	\color{gray}1 & \color{gray}-1 & \color{gray}3 
    \end{bmatrix}

    \begin{bmatrix}
        \color{gray}-5 & \color{red}2  \\
	\color{gray}2 & \color{red}-2  \\
	\color{gray}-4 & \color{red}1
    \end{bmatrix} \\ &=\begin{bmatrix}
        10 & \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}-7}}  \\
        \ast & \ast
    \end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}-7}} = ({\color{#4169E1}{-2}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{#4169E1}{2}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{#4169E1}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}
$$

To find $c_{21}$, we calculate the dot product of the second row of $A$ and the first column of $B$:

$$
\begin{aligned}&\begin{bmatrix}
        \color{gray}-2 & \color{gray}2 & \color{gray}1 \\
	\color{#4169E1}1 & \color{#4169E1}-1 & \color{#4169E1}3 
    \end{bmatrix}

    \begin{bmatrix}
         \color{red}-5 & \color{gray}2  \\
	\color{red}2 & \color{gray}-2  \\
	\color{red}-4 & \color{gray}1
    \end{bmatrix} \\ &=\begin{bmatrix}
        10 & -7  \\
        \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}-19}} & \ast
    \end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}-19}} = {\color{#4169E1}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\color{#4169E1}{-1}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{#4169E1}{3}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}})
$$

Finally, to find $c_{22}$, we calculate the dot product of the second row of $A$ and the second column of $B$:

$$
\begin{aligned}&\begin{bmatrix}
        \color{gray}-2 & \color{gray}2 & \color{gray}1 \\
	\color{#4169E1}1 & \color{#4169E1}-1 & \color{#4169E1}3 
    \end{bmatrix}

    \begin{bmatrix}
        \color{gray}-5 & \color{red}2  \\
	\color{gray}2 & \color{red}-2  \\
	\color{gray}-4 & \color{red}1
    \end{bmatrix} \\ &=\begin{bmatrix}
        10 & -7  \\
        -19 & \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}7}}
    \end{bmatrix}\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\color{black}7}} = {\color{#4169E1}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + ({\color{#4169E1}{-1}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{#4169E1}{3}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}
$$

Therefore, we have

$$
AB = 
	\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 \\ -19 & 7\end{bmatrix}.
$$
